WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.300
We're gonna do randomised Fibonacci sequences. So, we're gonna take a Fibonacci sequence, we're gonna randomise it ....with this coin.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.500
Now, I think we should first do a recap of Fibonacci sequences first, just to see what the original Fibonacci sequence is.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.580
So, your Fibonacci sequence

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:22.920
starts with a 1, and a 1, and then the next term is the sum of the previous two.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.390
We're gonna add those together

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:29.070
So 1 + 1 makes 2, 1 + 2 makes 3,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.520
2 and 3 make 5, 8, 13,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.309
21 - and so on.

00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:37.679
So, if you want to write that down as a little formula

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.829
You'd write it like this: if you want to find the next Fibonacci number, you just add the previous two

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:44.550
Okay

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.180
So, couple of well-known facts about Fibonacci numbers as well;

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:54.419
If you take two consecutive Fibonacci numbers, and we'll divide the big one by the smaller one,

00:00:54.699 --> 00:01:02.099
then you'll see, and it's been proven for a long time, that this will tend to a number, which is:

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:10.000
1.618033988...something, something, something

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.379
And people will know that this is a special number,

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:19.680
This is called the golden ratio, which is this symbol, the golden ratio. But you can use that to...estimate.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.740
Let's say you've a really big Fibonacci number and

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.799
You don't know what it is - you can use the golden ratio.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:32.610
So the idea is, every time, we're sort of multiplying by the golden ratio to get the next one up

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:37.200
Less true at the beginning, becomes more true the biggest these numbers are

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.930
(Brady: so you can, kind of like, reverse engineer your numbers?)

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.590
Yeah, yeah, you can work out -

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.270
the millionth

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.340
Fibonacci number, right

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:51.210
So if you wanted to work out the millionth Fibonacci number, what you're doing is multiplying by the golden ratio - a million times.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.220
So, let's say, approximately

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.030
equal to

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:58.680
golden ratio, which is this symbol, to a million. And I can tell you what that is -

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:07.500
I've actually worked this out - 4.4 x 10, and it's going to be a huge number: 208,987

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:13.800
So this a is really big Fibonacci number, but this an estimate, 'cause it is a bit rough

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:18.500
If you wanna see what it actually is, the actual millionth Fibonacci number is...

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:26.300
1.95 x 10 to the power 208,987.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.860
So not quite there, but the size of these numbers is spot on. So that was just a little recap of Fibonacci.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:34.600
'Cause what I wanna do now is,

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.769
randomise this! We all, awh Fibonacci we know everything about it! Oh, boring, so predictable.

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:47.519
So now, let's use my coin to randomise this a little bit. With this idea, I'm gonna create

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:56.900
a random Fibonacci sequence, the next one is going to be the sum, or the difference, of the previous two numbers.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.949
So, we're gonna start in the same way, with a 1 and a 1. Now, what is the next value?

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.000
We're gonna use my coin to find out

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.250
(Brady: So, normally it would have been 2?)

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.200
So this is, this, if it was the ordinary Fibonacci sequence we would add these together,

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:15.360
we'd get a 2. I'm gonna flip my coin. I might take the sum, I might take the difference.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.100
So let's see, what it's going to be.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.900
(Brady: You've got negative on one side...Ahh yeah, okay.)

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.060
See I, I prepared. I came prepped!

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:30.449
Ready? All right, so let's do - is it a sum or difference...And I've got difference!

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.500
(Brady: Difference. Okay, so that's - naught is the next number?)

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:38.820
So zero is the next number. Let's do the next number. Is it going to be a sum or a difference?

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:45.750
It's gonna be a sum! So I've got 1 now, add to the previous two to give me, 1.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.709
Let's see what I get next, let's try it.

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:50.410
[slaps hand]

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:54.100
Okay, I got a sum so we're got back to...

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.400
another 1, there.
[slaps hand]

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.280
Okay, I got a plus - so that's a 2. Let's keep going, is it a sum or difference?

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:08.840
Okay, we've now got a difference, so we're gonna do 
2 - 1, which gives me 1. Let's do a few more.

00:04:09.269 --> 00:04:13.999
We've got a plus now so that's going to be, a 3. 2 and a 1.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.070
Oh, and a difference here - so 3 - 1 = 2. So it's unpredictable.

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.700
I don't know what's going to happen next. And, if you don't mind I want to really make that point.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:29.780
If I did it again...I'm going to get a different sequence. But is there anything I can say about them?

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.679
So, I don't know if these are going to - you know,

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.500
go off to infinity - like the Fibonacci sequence just gets bigger and bigger and bigger, goes off to infinity.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.900
I don't know if it's going to go to - maybe it just goes off to minus infinity, I don't know that

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:48.500
Maybe it, kind of hovers around zero? Maybe the pluses and minuses cancel out.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.760
Or maybe it's unpredictable, maybe it's just chaotic and I don't know what's going to happen next.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:58.399
So the surprise is - I can make some predictions. I can make some long term predictions,

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:05.959
for example, I can tell you what the millionth random Fibonacci number is going to be

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:08.550
because

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:13.609
like the original Fibonacci sequence, there is a growth ratio.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.289
Just the same way,

00:05:15.289 --> 00:05:28.100
I want to show you that, so it's:
1.1319882487943....something, something, something.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.510
So this is our constant and it's going on forever. Which means we can use this

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:40.220
to predict the millionth random number. So, let's do it. Random Fibonacci number one million.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:46.400
Same kinda thing that I did with the Fibonacci numbers. It's gonna be, approximately,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:53.539
this constant: oh, I'll just do 1.319...you get the idea. To the power: one million

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.380
Which is:

00:05:55.380 --> 00:06:02.700
some big number.
8.3 x 10 to the 53,841

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:07.400
I will tell you, this is a statement about the size of the number - not the sign of the number.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:13.130
So I don't know if it's a plus or a minus number. So it might be a plus or minus.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:18.679
So this might be going off to positive infinity, and there's no reason why it can't go down to negative infinity;

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:23.270
but they are growing, and the size of these numbers are growing,

00:06:23.639 --> 00:06:29.209
and, in the long term, we can predict how much it will grow by. If you wanna put it another way,

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.300
you could say it like this:

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:38.059
If you take the nth random Fibonacci number, and make it positive, and we took the nth root,

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:47.000
this is tending towards our magic number here. 1.1319 and stuff, as

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:49.639
n tends to infinity.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.710
Although, well I'll put a little thing here

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.900
"Almost surely"

00:06:54.900 --> 00:07:01.699
That's a funny expression I've slipped in. This tends to this constant "almost surely"

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.759
Because the original Fibonacci sequence

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:10.819
would count as a randomised Fibonacci sequence *if* I was just getting plus

00:07:11.219 --> 00:07:13.609
every time I flipped my coin: plus, plus, plus, plus.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:20.209
So, that counts and that has a growth rate which is the golden ratio - it's not this value.

00:07:20.819 --> 00:07:26.778
But the chances of me getting plus, plus, plus; over and over again so that I've got the Fibonacci sequence

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:29.389
becomes increasing small.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.930
Err, so if you look at all the possible random Fibonacci sequences

00:07:33.719 --> 00:07:36.079
things like the original Fibonacci sequence

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:44.120
almost never happens. I'm not going to say it's impossible, but it's *almost* never going to happen. If you did this for real,

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.800
almost 100% of the time you're getting a

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:54.469
properly random sequence of pluses and minuses which tend towards this growth constant.

00:07:55.199 --> 00:07:59.869
Another example of what wouldn't work, is if I had a pattern. If I had something like

00:08:00.509 --> 00:08:06.649
plus, minus, minus as a pattern, and it went plus, minus, minus. So if I started with a 1 and a 1

00:08:07.949 --> 00:08:10.818
it would be plus, minus, minus as a pattern

00:08:11.159 --> 00:08:17.809
which would go plus, would make a 2. Then a minus; would be 2 - 1, so that should be a 1.

00:08:18.479 --> 00:08:22.429
Then a minus again  1 - 2, I've got -1

00:08:23.159 --> 00:08:25.699
Then we go for a plus which gives me a 0

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.430
Then we'll do a minus

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:33.179
which is going to give me a 1 and another minus gives me a 1. And I get back

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:40.140
to the original two numbers and then that pattern will continue, so that's not growing at all.

00:08:40.479 --> 00:08:45.538
That's just oscillating up and down, so that doesn't have a growth rate, because it's not growing anywhere.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:49.890
You won't really see this coming out though, until you're using...

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:53.939
Until you've done this for about...two hundred thousand? times

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:59.280
So you have to go quite far and then you start to see that this starts to tend to these numbers.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:05.100
(Brady: You don't think someone watching is going to make some computer simulator that will do this two hundred thousand times?)

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:05.760
And that's what they did!

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.440
That's exactly what they've done.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.960
So when they were trying to calculate these numbers they simulated it. And they could see that in the long term,

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:15.300
this was the growth rate.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:21.510
In the 60s they showed that these randomised Fibonacci sequences do grow.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:24.719
I think that's a surprise in itself because, you know,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.660
they've got pluses and minuses don't really cancel out, turns out they don't, so these sequences are growing.

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:35.009
So they could prove that it was growing, but they didn't know what the growth rate was.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:38.400
So, that took, awh till

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:43.770
1999, so we're talking 30 years later

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:48.300
and, it was calculated what this growth rate was, and that, was really hard.

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:54.390
Proper advanced mathematics, proper advanced computing; but what they did calculate was

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:57.179
this part of it:

00:09:57.179 --> 00:10:03.200
1.13198824

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.700
and that's as far as they could go 'cause it's really hard to calculate. And that was 20 years ago.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:15.719
And in the 20 years since, we've worked out 5 more digits and that's as far as we've got.

